<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Adaptive icon foreground: a four-pane window.

    "Darcha" means *little window*, and a four-pane window is already a
    spreadsheet grid — one shape, two readings. The panes are deliberately
    unequal: a short top row and a narrow left column, the proportions a
    spreadsheet has because of its header row and row-number column. An evenly
    quartered window would read as a window and nothing more.

    THE FOREGROUND IS ARTWORK ONLY. It draws no outer shape of its own — the
    silhouette comes from the launcher's mask. The previous version's frame ran
    to r≈38.5 from centre, past even the 36 the mask can show, so circle and
    One UI masks shaved its corners and the white frame became the icon's
    outline instead of a window inside a field.

    SAFE ZONE, measured rather than assumed. The canvas is 108 with centre
    (54,54); the mask shows at most r=36 and the guidance is to stay inside
    r=33. The outermost point here is a corner arc:

        arc centre (37,40) -> offset (17,14) -> r = 22.02
        + corner radius 5 + half stroke 3     = r = 30.02

    That leaves ~3 units of room inside the recommended circle and ~6 inside the
    hard limit. Nothing else comes close: the straight edges reach r=25 and 22.

    STROKE WEIGHTS. The canvas maps its centre 72 units onto the icon, so one
    unit is size/72 dp. At a 48dp icon that is 0.667dp, which is 0.667px on an
    mdpi screen:

        2 units -> 1.33 px   sub-pixel; aliases into a smudge
        3 units -> 2.00 px   the floor for a crisp line
        6 units -> 4.00 px

    Hence frame 6, mullions 3 — a 2:1 ratio that survives the smallest size
    while keeping the frame visibly heavier, which is how both real windows and
    real spreadsheets are drawn. No data lines inside the panes: two 2-unit
    strokes would be 1.33px each at 48dp and collapse into one mark.

    PALETTE. Deep indigo ground, white joinery, one warm pane. Measured
    contrast: white on ground 12.1:1, amber on ground 5.0:1, amber against the
    white that borders it 2.4:1 — enough to keep the pane's edges legible
    without the amber competing with the frame.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

    <!--
        One pane lit, the way a selected cell is — and it is the top-left one,
        because that is where a spreadsheet starts. Drawn first so the frame and
        mullions land on top of it and it fills its cell exactly.
    -->
    <path
        android:pathData="M35,38 H49 V49 H35 Z"
        android:fillColor="#F0912A" />

    <!-- The window frame: 44 x 38, stroked at 6. -->
    <path
        android:pathData="M37,35 H71 A5,5 0 0 1 76,40 V68 A5,5 0 0 1 71,73 H37 A5,5 0 0 1 32,68 V40 A5,5 0 0 1 37,35 Z"
        android:strokeColor="#FFFFFF"
        android:strokeWidth="6"
        android:fillColor="#00000000"
        android:strokeLineJoin="round" />

    <!--
        The mullions, at 3 — half the frame. They do not cross at the centre:
        the interior is 38 x 32, split 14:21 across and 11:18 down, so the
        crossing sits up and to the left at (50.5, 50.5). That offset is the
        whole spreadsheet reading.
    -->
    <path
        android:pathData="M50.5,35 V73"
        android:strokeColor="#FFFFFF"
        android:strokeWidth="3" />
    <path
        android:pathData="M32,50.5 H76"
        android:strokeColor="#FFFFFF"
        android:strokeWidth="3" />
</vector>
